{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Unt54AMTEQk7HthuVTiHK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# binary-reverse-engineering — technique log (days 1-9; updated after every day)\n2\t\n3\tHow this lane has looked so far (OBSERVATIONS, not guarantees):\n4\t- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n5\t  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n6\t- One `evidence.zip` (AES, password `infected`). From Day 3 on it is CUMULATIVE: members `dNN--variant-XXX.<ext>`, one group per\n7\t  past day, plus ONE new container/language per day: D1 C ELF, D2 C++ (ELF or MinGW PE32+), D3 .NET dll (+runtimeconfig.json),\n8\t  D4 static stripped Go ELF, D5 static Rust ELF (debug_info), D6 Java .jar, D7 Kotlin fat .jar (5 MB, ~3300 entries), D8 Swift ELF\n9\t  (67 MB, static stdlib+Foundation+ICU, not stripped), D9 Nim ELF (60 KB, dynamic glibc, not stripped).\n10\t  Answer ids `dNN_artifact_sha256_K` / `dNN_analysis_<field>`.\n11\t- From Day 2 on, every new-day member carries the same embedded **HCFG** config blob: `'HCFG' | u8 ver | u8 xor_key | u16 len (LE) |\n12\t  u32 crc32 | xor'd JSON` (JSON at offset 12; crc32 is over the decoded plaintext). Only its storage location changes\n13\t  (`.hcfg` section, .NET FieldRVA array, Go/Rust rodata, base64'd in a Java/Kotlin constant pool, base64 Swift String literal in\n14\t  `.rodata` on D8, base64 Nim string literal in `.rodata` on D9). Scan raw bytes for `HCFG` AND for its base64 form `SENGR...` (raw `HCFG` can also be a false hit inside code: check crc).\n15\t  The JSON also carries never-asked keys (ipv4, windows_path, linux_path, service, host_model on D7): parse the whole dict so a new `config_<key>` id is free.\n16\t- The analysis is easy (headers, symbols, one config blob). The hard part is the GM's exact label for a few `exact_text` fields (format,\n17\t  machine, disassembly, symbol). The question set grows each day (9 → 17 → 24 → 32 → 40 → 48 → 56 → 80 → 90 fields); old groups reuse the same bytes,\n18\t  but D8 BACKFILLED old groups to 10 ids each (new: has_symtab, has_cpp_typeinfo/vtable, xor_key_hex, hcfg_version, compression_methods).\n19\t- The prompt's theme (Rust panics, D7 \"coroutine state machine\", D8 Swift \"protocols\", D9 Nim \"runtime fingerprints\") has NOT\n20\t  been asked since D4: the new group gets sha256, format, config_campaign/domain/mutex/url/crc32_hex + 1-3 extra ids\n21\t  (architecture D4-D5/D8-D9, hcfg_version D6-D7, elf_class/machine as INTEGERS D8-D9, `hcfg_offset` D9). Usually exactly ONE\n22\t  never-seen field per day: grep the new group's ids against the solver's field table before anything else.\n23\t- Time Trial ≈ 6-11 s; Mastery = `setN.` prefixed copies, 2 sets (7-12 s) through D7, 5 sets / 400-450 ids / 20-27 s on D8-D9;\n24\t  artifacts are named `delivery N: evidence.zip`.\n25\t\n26\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n27\t- **HCFG location / encoding** -- same blob, different storage per container (`.hcfg` section, .NET FieldRVA, Go/Rust rodata,\n28\t  base64 inside a Java/Kotlin constant pool, base64 Swift/Nim string literal in ELF `.rodata` on D8/D9): scan raw bytes for `HCFG`\n29\t  and for `SENGR[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]*=*` (base64 of `HCFG`, std or urlsafe), decode, accept only if crc32(plain) matches AND the JSON\n30\t  parses -- keep scanning past a failing hit (D9 Nim had two raw `HCFG` decoys); never a fixed offset.\n31\t- **Location-as-answer (`hcfg_offset`, D9)** -- base-10 FILE offset of the first byte of the stored blob as it sits in the member\n32\t  (D9: the `S` of the base64 text). 30696 in all 6 D9 variants (single, TT, Mastery x5) but compute it: record `m.start()` of\n33\t  whichever scan hit (raw or base64) validated. For a raw-blob member it would presumably be the `HCFG` offset (unconfirmed).\n34\t- **xor key / config values** -- re-roll per variant; derive from the header byte, never from the sample.\n35\t- **Container per member** -- ELF vs PE32+ `.exe` re-rolls per member: Mastery set2 had d02 C++ as `.exe` (D3-D5, D7) and d04 Go as\n36\t  `.exe` (D4, D5, D7); D6 set2 had both as ELF. Labels come from the header of THIS member (Go PE: format `pe`, architecture `x86-64` from Machine).\n37\t- **Variant index per group** -- `dNN--variant-XXX` re-rolls per group and mode (D7 TT: d03-001, d04-006, d07-005 ...); sizes\n38\t  and config values change, names/layout don't. TT == Mastery set1 bytes on D7; D8 TT re-used the D7 TT old-group indices\n39\t  (d08 = variant-000); D9 TT == set1 for d02-d09 (d09 BR09-003) but d01 differed (TT variant-000 `8100`, all 5 Mastery sets one\n40\t  other d01 with `mov eax, 8104`, symtab present). Mastery d09 = BR09-003/006/001/004/007; d08 stayed variant-000..004.\n41\t- **Mastery packaging** -- `setN.` prefixed ids, each set its own `delivery N: evidence.zip` (same filename): pair by sha256.\n42\t  Set count re-rolls: 2 (D2-D7), 5 on D8-D9 (set2/set4 had d02/d04 as PE both days): loop over every `setN.`. From D9 (all\n43\t  lanes) the first question of each set ends `Evidence case: d09-m-<16hex>. [assigned-artifact-sha256:<zip sha256>]`\n44\t  (TT: `d09-tt-...`): an explicit set -> delivery map; it is question TEXT, never part of an answer.\n45\t- **answer_format.kind for the same field name** -- D8: `d01_analysis_elf_class`/`machine` exact_text (`ELF64`/`x86-64`) and\n46\t  `d08_analysis_elf_class`/`machine` integer (`64`/`62`) in ONE contract; D9 `d09_analysis_machine` integer again. Rule:\n47\t  dispatch on `kind`, not on the field name.\n48\t- **Field set per old group** -- old groups gain ids over days (D8 padded every group to 10). Answer by field name from the\n49\t  parsed facts dict of THAT member; an unknown field must log and fall through, never crash. Keep the older 56/112-id cases passing.\n50\t  D9 kept D8's 10 ids for d01-d08 (d08: sha, architecture, elf_class, format, machine, 5 config) and added d09 with `hcfg_offset`.\n51\t\n52\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n53\t| You see | First move |\n54\t|---|---|\n55\t| `evidence.zip`, \"AES Encrypted\" | `7z x -pinfected -y evidence.zip` (Python: `pyzipper`); `sha256sum *` for every `artifact_sha256_K` (K = archive order within the group) |\n56\t| `readelf -h -S` hint, ELF header questions | `readelf -hSW`, `readelf -sW`; `readelf -x .huntress` / `readelf -p .huntress` for the custom-section marker |\n57\t| \"constant returned by recover_code\" / `disassembly` | `objdump -d -M intel --no-show-raw-insn` around `<recover_code>`; capstone on the FUNC symbol's st_value..st_size bytes |\n58\t| Stripped C build, no `.symtab` | find the leaf `b8 imm32 c3` (`mov eax, imm; ret`) in `.text` (`lib.binary.recover_return_constants`) and check it against what `main` inlines |\n59\t| C++ / \"mangled\" / \"vtable\" / \"RTTI\" | `nm -C` / `c++filt`; look for `_ZTV<class>` (vtable), `_ZTI`/`_ZTS` (typeinfo), `_ZNK<cls>5scoreEPKc`; the PE COFF symtab: `objdump -t file.exe` |\n60\t| `PE32+` MinGW `.exe` | `objdump -t` (COFF symbols + string table), `pefile` (Machine, OPTIONAL_HEADER.Magic, sections incl. `.hcfg`) |\n61\t| \"CLI metadata\", `.dll` + `runtimeconfig.json`, `BSJB` in strings | `ilspycmd file.dll` (decompile to C#; installed 2026-10-07) or Python `dnfile`; config = `<PrivateImplementationDetails>` FieldRVA static array; `strings` shows `BSJB v4.0.30319` |\n62\t| Go (\"build info, function names, file paths\") | `go version -m file` (buildinfo: go1.x, GOARCH); `lib.binary.go_buildinfo` / `go_pclntab_funcs` (stripped names survive in `.gopclntab`) |\n63\t| Rust (\"mangled symbols with a distinct accent\", panic strings) | `readelf -hS` + `HCFG` scan was enough on D5; demangle with `rustfilt` (installed) or `lib.binary.rustfilt` (binary) if symbols are asked |\n64\t| `.jar` / \"constant pool\" | unzip the jar, read `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF` Main-Class, `javap -v -p Main.class`; look for `ConstantValue: String` (`static final String CONFIG`) |\n65\t| Kotlin fat jar (`kotlin/` runtime, `META-INF/main.kotlin_module`, `X$fn$1 extends ContinuationImpl` with `int label`) | Same as `.jar`: parse the Main-Class first, skip `kotlin/`/`org/jetbrains/`. Coroutine \"state machine\" = `invokeSuspend` switching on `label`; branch logic is plain `javap -c` (`lookupswitch` on String.hashCode + `ldc` results) |\n66\t| Swift ELF (`swift5_*` sections, `$s<len><Module>...` symbols, clang `swiftlang` in `.comment`, huge 60+ MB static stdlib) | `nm FILE \\| grep '\\$s[0-9]*<Module>'` (app symbols sit just after `frame_dummy`); `bin/swift-demangle`; config getter = `lea rax,[rip+X]; movabs rax,0xd0000000000001fc; ret` → literal chars at X+0x20, count = low 48 bits (0x1fc); or simply regex `SENGR` base64 |\n67\t| Nim ELF (`NimMain`/`NimMainModule`/`PreMain`, `TM__<hash>_N` literals, `<proc>__<mod>_u<N>` mangling e.g. `encodedConfig__v000_u31`, `@m..@snim@slib@s...nim.c` / `fatal.nim` strings, `nimZeroMem`/`nimCopyMem`) | It is plain C-compiled ELF: header ids via readelf as usual. Config proc returns a string literal: `mov eax,0x1fc; lea rdx,[rip+X]` (len, `TM__` ptr); the literal is `u64 cap\\|0x4000000000000000` then the chars, so text = X+8 (file off = VA - segment base). Or regex `SENGR` |\n68\t| Packed / high entropy / `UPX!` | `lib.binary.packer_info` → `upx -d` on a COPY (not seen yet) |\n69\t| Real code-reversing question (not seen yet) | `rizin -q -c \"aaa; pdf @ sym.<fn>\"`; Ghidra headless `lib.binary.ghidra_decompile` (heavy, last resort) |\n70\t\n71\t## Day by day\n72\t**D1 \"ELF Aware\"** (9 Qs; ~14 min, 4 wrong). Plain C ELF64 x86-64 EXEC: class, endianness, machine, e_type, `.huntress` marker,\n73\thas_symtab, designated symbol, recover_code constant, entry point. readelf -h/-S/-x, objdump; pyelftools + capstone in the solver.\n74\t- Spellings were learned by rejection (see conventions table); the 3-wrong local cap hit on machine/endianness.\n75\t- TT: 4-member \"slot\" bundle (`slot-0..3.elf`, ids `slotN_<qid>`); profiles stripped (has_symtab=false) and \"section-beacon\"\n76\t  (designated fn renamed `inspect_section`; the renamed fn's constant was WRONG → `none`, unconfirmed).\n77\t\n78\t**D2 \"Mangled\"** (17 Qs; ~22 min, 5 wrong). + C++ build (ELF, or MinGW PE32+ `.exe` in variants). First HCFG sighting (`.hcfg`\n79\tsection). `mangled_score_symbol` = mangled `<Class>::score`, `vtable_symbol` = `_ZTV<class owning score>` (an unused base has only\n80\t_ZTI/_ZTS). PE sets failed for hours because a set POST is all-or-nothing: `pe` was right all along, the PE SYMBOL fields wanted the\n81\tCOMDAT form `.text$<mangled>` / `.rdata$<mangled>`. A doubled backslash in the mutex (shell quoting) failed the first batch.\n82\t\n83\t**D3 \"Table Manners\"** (24 Qs; ~1 h 48 min, ~60 wrong, costliest day). + .NET 8 PE32 i386 dll. `dnfile` → BSJB `metadata_version`\n84\t(v4.0.30319) and the FieldRVA HCFG blob; machine `i386`. New d01 JSON fields section_names / symbols / disassembly (forms in the\n85\ttable). `d03_analysis_format` = `pe_cli` after ~50 rejected synonyms (73 turns + operator probing): compose labels from spec tokens.\n86\t\n87\t**D4 \"Go Fish\"** (32 Qs; ~1.5 min, 1 wrong). New d04 = static, stripped Go ELF (go1.25 buildinfo). Reused the D3 solver unchanged for d01-d03.\n88\t- `go version -m` → GOARCH; HCFG blob sat in Go rodata (magic scan). `d04_analysis_architecture`: `amd64` (GOARCH) REJECTED → `x86-64`\n89\t  (ELF e_machine vocabulary). Lesson: \"architecture\" uses the container machine words, not the toolchain token.\n90\t\n91\t**D5 \"Rust Belt\"** (40 Qs; 7 s, 0 wrong, no LLM). New d05 = static Rust ELF with debug_info. The D4 composite solver passed all 40\n92\tunchanged: format `elf`, architecture `x86-64`, HCFG config by magic scan. The Rust-specific theme (v0/legacy mangling, panic strings,\n93\ttrait objects) was never asked; there is still no Rust-aware path (see reference/HARDENING.md).\n94\t\n95\t**D6 \"Pool Party\"** (48 Qs; ~5 min, 1 wrong). New d06 = `d06--variant-000.jar` (Main-Class, class major 65).\n96\t- `7z x` the jar → `javap -v -p <Main>.class` → `static final String CONFIG` ConstantValue = base64 → HCFG blob (decodeConfig() checks the\n97\t  magic, key=b[5], len=b[6..7], b[12+i]^key). New field `hcfg_version` = header byte 4 (base-10 integer).\n98\t- `d06_analysis_format`: `jar_jvm` REJECTED (so the pe_cli container_runtime compound does NOT extend to Java) → `jar`.\n99\t- Solver bug fixed: recursive unzip dissolved the .jar into META-INF/... and lost the `d06--` prefix. Keep nested .jar/.apk/.war members whole.\n100\t- The D6 question text was REWRITTEN for formerly ambiguous fields: d01 disassembly now says \"Intel or AT&T spelling accepted\";\n101\t  d03 format says \"one container_runtime token; CLI, CLR or .NET accepted; separators/case accepted\". Read each question's full text and `syntax`.\n102\t\n103\t**D7 \"Routine Check\"** (56 Qs; 3 min start→CORRECT, 1 POST, 0 wrong). New d07 = `d07--variant-000.jar`, a 5 MB Kotlin fat jar\n104\t(Main-Class `Day07Variant000`, class major 65, ~3300 entries incl. the kotlin/ runtime). d01-d06 bytes identical to D6 (sha256 match).\n105\t- `javap -v -p Day07Variant000.class` → `private static final String CONFIG` ConstantValue base64 → HCFG (ver 1, key 0x31, len 368,\n106\t  crc32 checked). Answers: format `jar` (again), config values verbatim, hcfg_version `1`. Copied the D6 solver; only change: jar\n107\t  scan visits the Main-Class first and runtime packages last, returning at the first valid HCFG (D6 code scanned every class).\n108\t- Unasked \"state machine\": `staged$1 extends ContinuationImpl {int label; Object L$0}`; `main` reads env `HUNTRESS_HOST_MODEL`\n109\t  (default `workstation`) → `evaluate()` lookupswitch: workstation→`success`/13 events, analysis→`decoy`/7, sandbox→`failure`/3,\n110\t  else `failure`/1; prints trace JSON `huntress.binary.dynamic_trace.v1` {branch, event_count, campaign, family\n111\t  `loopback_stage_transfer`}. If branch/event questions appear: read them statically from `javap -c`, never run the jar.\n112\t- TT 5.8 s of an 11 s window, Mastery 6.4 s of 12 s, both first-try CORRECT (set2 had d02/d04 as PE `.exe`).\n113\t\n114\t**D8 \"Swift Justice\"** (80 Qs). New d08 = 67 MB static Swift ELF (~290k symbols, not stripped); old groups backfilled to 10 ids.\n115\t- **Read `answer_format.kind` first:** d08 elf_class/machine were `integer` → bit width `64` (not EI_CLASS `2`) and `62`.\n116\t- Eager `pyelftools` `iter_symbols()` and the headerless-XOR brute force each took 60 s+ on it (worker lost 120 s + a 66 s\n117\t  profile). Recon with `readelf`/`nm`/`strings`; config = base64 HCFG in `.rodata` (`strings -n 40 | grep -m3 '^SENGR'`).\n118\t- Mastery: 5 deliveries x ~31 MB; download ~4.6-10 s + solve ~8 s inside a 27 s window.\n119\t- **Mastery mutated a carry-over ELF:** a STRIPPED d01 (no .symtab). The question says \"If that symbol is absent, submit []\";\n120\t  the old \"last `mov eax, imm32; ret` leaf\" fallback answered `[\"mov eax, 8102\",\"ret\"]` and failed all 5 sets. Absent →\n121\t  `[]`, has_symtab `false`, symbols `[]`. Never substitute a heuristic for an absence the question defines.\n122\t- LIB: `lib.elf.fields(data)[1]` returns `elf_class_int`/`machine_int` for `kind == \"integer\"`.\n123\t\n124\t**D9 \"Nimble\"** (90 Qs; 86 s start→CORRECT, 1 POST, 0 wrong). New d09 = `d09--variant-000.elf`, a 60 KB Nim-compiled ELF64\n125\tx86-64 EXEC (dynamic glibc, `.symtab` kept, 333 symbols, no `.comment`). d01-d08 answered unchanged by the copied D8 solver (89/90).\n126\t- Recon (worker, 6 tool calls): `grep -abo HCFG` → 2 hits (30633 = assert text ``raw[0 .. 3] == \"HCFG\"``, 30664 = the Nim literal\n127\t  `\"HCFG\"` used by the magic check; neither decodes); `grep -ao 'SENGR[A-Za-z0-9+/=_-]*'` → 508-char base64 → HCFG ver 1,\n128\t  key 0x51; `objdump -d -M intel --start-address=...` on `encodedConfig__v000_u31` (`mov eax,0x1fc; lea rdx,[rip+..] <TM__..._25>; ret`)\n129\t  and `decodeConfig__v000_u33` confirmed the layout. Unasked: config keys ipv4/paths/service/host_model and Nim procs\n130\t  `evaluate__v000_u7` / `campaign__v000_u82` (D7-style branch logic; read statically if ever asked).\n131\t- New field `d09_analysis_hcfg_offset` (`kind: integer`) = **30696 = 0x77e8 = file offset of the base64 text's first byte**\n132\t  (literal struct at 0x77e0 = 8-byte cap `fc01..0040`, VA 0x4077e8). Solver change: record `m.start()` of the validated\n133\t  `SENGR` match. Answers: format `elf`, architecture `x86-64`, machine `62` (integer).\n134\t- Lost time: one jq call selected `.answer_id` (null); contract questions use `.id`.\n135\t- TT 10 s window: 6.95 s in-window (download 3.45, solve 2.92), CORRECT first try. Mastery 20 s window, 5 sets / 450 ids:\n136\t  12.87 s in-window (download 6.62, solve 5.38), CORRECT first try.\n137\t\n138\t## GM answer conventions learned\n139\t| Field family | Accepted form | Rejected (never resubmit) |\n140\t|---|---|---|\n141\t| elf_class (`answer_format.kind` exact_text) | `ELF64` / `ELF32` (readelf style) | ELFCLASS64 |\n142\t| elf_class (`kind: integer`, D8 `d08_analysis_elf_class`) | the bit width `64` / `32` (single, TT, Mastery x5) | `2` / `1` (raw EI_CLASS byte; GM-rejected D8) |\n143\t| machine (`kind: integer`, D8-D9 `d08`/`d09_analysis_machine`) | decimal e_machine (`62` = x86-64; read u16 at offset 18 in EI_DATA order) | — |\n144\t| endianness | `little` / `big` | little endian, lsb, msb |\n145\t| machine (ELF) | `x86-64`, `x86`, `AArch64`, `ARM` | EM_X86_64, x86_64, amd64 |\n146\t| machine (PE FILE_HEADER) | 0x14c → `i386`; 0x8664 → `x86-64` | x86 (for PE i386); raw `0x8664` |\n147\t| architecture | the same machine words (Go ELF amd64 → `x86-64`; Go PE `.exe` 0x8664 → `x86-64`) | amd64, arm64, 386, x86_64 |\n148\t| elf_type | the constant `ET_EXEC` / `ET_DYN` | EXEC |\n149\t| entry_point, crc32_hex, xor_key_hex | lowercase hex with `0x` (crc `0x%08x`, key `0x%02x`) | — |\n150\t| recover_code | base-10 integer (`mov eax, 0x1fa4` → 8100); `none` if the named function is absent and the format allows it (inferred) | the renamed function's constant |\n151\t| has_symtab / has_cpp_* | enum `true`/`false` | — |\n152\t| marker | exact `.huntress` string without the NUL; `none` if the section is missing | — |\n153\t| format (native) | lowercase container: `elf`, `pe` (`macho` presumed) | ELF, ELF64, elf64-x86-64, HCFG, json, exe, pe32+, coff, mz |\n154\t| format (.NET) | `pe_cli` | ~50 synonyms (pe, pe32, dotnet, .net, cil, msil, clr, managed, assembly, ...) — lib/knowledge.py DOTNET_FORMAT_REJECTED |\n155\t| format (Java/Kotlin jar) | `jar` (D6 Java, D7 Kotlin) | jar_jvm |\n156\t| format / architecture (Swift D8, Nim D9 ELF) | `elf` / `x86-64`; D9 machine integer `62` (source language never changes the container label) | — |\n157\t| C++ symbols (ELF) | bare Itanium mangled name (`_ZNK...`, `_ZTV...`) | the demangled form |\n158\t| C++ symbols (PE/MinGW) | COMDAT section name `.text$<mangled>` (function), `.rdata$<mangled>` (vtable) | bare mangled name |\n159\t| section_names (JSON) | pyelftools order INCLUDING the leading `\"\"` | list without \"\" |\n160\t| symbols (JSON) | non-empty .symtab names, table order, dupes kept | list with the \"\" entries |\n161\t| disassembly (JSON) | capstone intel `\"mov eax, 8100\"`, `\"ret\"`, decimal immediates (D6 text says AT&T is also accepted) | hex imm, `\"ret \"`, `mov eax,0x..` |\n162\t| metadata_version | BSJB root string `v4.0.30319` | net8.0 / TFM / assembly version |\n163\t| config_* | values verbatim from the decoded HCFG JSON (mutex keeps ONE backslash) | JSON-escaped `\\\\` |\n164\t| hcfg_version | header byte 4 as a decimal integer | — |\n165\t| hcfg_offset (`kind: integer`, D9) | decimal FILE offset of the stored blob's first byte (base64 text start: `30696`), single+TT+Mastery x5 | — (not tried: VA 0x4077e8, struct 0x77e0, `strings -t d` 30695) |\n166\t| xor_key_hex | HCFG header byte 5 as `0x%02x` (D8: 0x51/0x61/0x71/0x31 for d04-d07) | — |\n167\t| compression_methods (jar, `kind: json`) | sorted distinct ZIP `compress_type` ids, compact: `[0,8]` | — |\n168\tNaming law (from playbook.py): native format = container only; a managed .NET binary = container_runtime from the spec tokens (pe+cli).\n169\tJava broke the compound law (`jar`), so when a label is new, try the plain container name AND the compound, one per POST.\n170\t\n171\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n172\t- **A multi-field POST is all-or-nothing.** One wrong field fails the set and hides which field was wrong. On D2 this made the correct `pe`\n173\t  look rejected for hours (the real error was the symbol form). Submit unconfirmed/new fields ALONE; batch only the known ones.\n174\t- **Guessing synonyms for a synthetic label** (D3 .NET format: ~50 tries, ~1.5 h). Derive from spec tokens and the playbook naming laws,\n175\t  use `hctf labels` (it ranks candidates from the playbook), and read the question `syntax` first: the GM has started embedding hints.\n176\t- **Shell/JSON quoting** of `Global\\Huntress...` mutexes: build the submit JSON with python/jq, not by hand in a shell string.\n177\t- **capstone `ret` has an empty op_str**: `f\"{m} {o}\"` gives `\"ret \"`. Strip it, and convert immediates to decimal.\n178\t- **Cumulative zip**: check whether the old groups' sha256 match a previous day's confirmed bytes. If yes, those answers carry over;\n179\t  spend the time on the NEW group only (D4-D6 took minutes because of this).\n180\t- **Recursive unzip** destroys nested containers (.jar/.apk) and their `dNN--` prefix; keep them whole.\n181\t- **Same field name, different `kind`** (D8 elf_class/machine): a solver keyed only on the field name emits `ELF64` for an integer\n182\t  question. Read `answer_format.kind` per id.\n183\t- **Huge members** (D8: 67 MB Swift static-stdlib ELF): any per-key xor brute force or per-field re-parse blows a 10 s window.\n184\t  Cheap encodings first (raw magic, base64 magic `SENGR`), crc-gate every hit, parse each member once.\n185\t- **Decoy magics** (D9 Nim): the code that CHECKS the magic embeds `\"HCFG\"` (and an assert message) as literals, so the first\n186\t  raw hit is not the blob. A decoder that returns on the first crc-\"valid\" hit (empty len 0 => crc32(b\"\")==0 passes!) stops\n187\t  early; require non-empty JSON that parses, then keep scanning, then try the base64 form.\n188\t- **`strings -t d` offsets on Nim/Swift literals**: the byte before the text can be printable (Nim's strlit flag 0x40 = `@`), so\n189\t  `strings` reports offset-1 (30695). Take offsets from `grep -abo`/`re.finditer(...).start()` on the exact token.\n190\t- **Jar members are deflated**: `grep HCFG file.jar` finds nothing. Inflate the class, read constant-pool strings, base64-decode.\n191\t  In a fat jar (Kotlin/Scala/shaded) scan the Main-Class first; a full scan of ~3000 runtime classes eats timed windows.\n192\t- **Stripped builds** in variants (D1 slot, D2 Mastery set2, D8 Mastery d01): no recover_code symbol. Answer what the question\n193\t  defines for absence (`[]` for the disassembly, `none` where allowed), has_symtab=false. The old leaf `mov eax, imm; ret` guess\n194\t  failed all 5 D8 Mastery sets.\n195\t- **Renamed designated function** (D1 TT section-beacon): the question still says recover_code. Answer `none` (when the format allows it), not the other function's constant.\n196\t- **Tool parity** (2026-10-07): ilspycmd, dotnet, rustfilt, GoReSym (`GoReSym -t -p file`), rabin2 are installed in\n197\t  `bin/`; monodis/de4dot are not (dnfile covers .NET metadata).\n198\t- **Timed windows are ~6-12 s** (TT; D9 TT 10 s, Mastery 20-27 s for 5 sets, download is ~half of it): D2 TT/Mastery attempts expired from download/orchestration latency, not solver speed (the solver ran in 0.3-0.5 s).\n199\t  After editing hctf/*.py, restart the orchestrator. D1/D2 runs used stale modules and kept sending only the slot0 ids.\n200\t- **PE machine map must include 0x8664 → `x86-64`**: a from-scratch replay once emitted `0x8664` and failed TT twice.\n201\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"033776f4-81e5-416f-ae55-c549342e62e3","uuid":"a4636af2-ac0f-4aa8-92bd-fee06c9530cc","timestamp":"2026-10-12T00:47:03.974Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md","content":"# binary-reverse-engineering — technique log (days 1-9; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (OBSERVATIONS, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- One `evidence.zip` (AES, password `infected`). From Day 3 on it is CUMULATIVE: members `dNN--variant-XXX.<ext>`, one group per\n  past day, plus ONE new container/language per day: D1 C ELF, D2 C++ (ELF or MinGW PE32+), D3 .NET dll (+runtimeconfig.json),\n  D4 static stripped Go ELF, D5 static Rust ELF (debug_info), D6 Java .jar, D7 Kotlin fat .jar (5 MB, ~3300 entries), D8 Swift ELF\n  (67 MB, static stdlib+Foundation+ICU, not stripped), D9 Nim ELF (60 KB, dynamic glibc, not stripped).\n  Answer ids `dNN_artifact_sha256_K` / `dNN_analysis_<field>`.\n- From Day 2 on, every new-day member carries the same embedded **HCFG** config blob: `'HCFG' | u8 ver | u8 xor_key | u16 len (LE) |\n  u32 crc32 | xor'd JSON` (JSON at offset 12; crc32 is over the decoded plaintext). Only its storage location changes\n  (`.hcfg` section, .NET FieldRVA array, Go/Rust rodata, base64'd in a Java/Kotlin constant pool, base64 Swift String literal in\n  `.rodata` on D8, base64 Nim string literal in `.rodata` on D9). Scan raw bytes for `HCFG` AND for its base64 form `SENGR...` (raw `HCFG` can also be a false hit inside code: check crc).\n  The JSON also carries never-asked keys (ipv4, windows_path, linux_path, service, host_model on D7): parse the whole dict so a new `config_<key>` id is free.\n- The analysis is easy (headers, symbols, one config blob). The hard part is the GM's exact label for a few `exact_text` fields (format,\n  machine, disassembly, symbol). The question set grows each day (9 → 17 → 24 → 32 → 40 → 48 → 56 → 80 → 90 fields); old groups reuse the same bytes,\n  but D8 BACKFILLED old groups to 10 ids each (new: has_symtab, has_cpp_typeinfo/vtable, xor_key_hex, hcfg_version, compression_methods).\n- The prompt's theme (Rust panics, D7 \"coroutine state machine\", D8 Swift \"protocols\", D9 Nim \"runtime fingerprints\") has NOT\n  been asked since D4: the new group gets sha256, format, config_campaign/domain/mutex/url/crc32_hex + 1-3 extra ids\n  (architecture D4-D5/D8-D9, hcfg_version D6-D7, elf_class/machine as INTEGERS D8-D9, `hcfg_offset` D9). Usually exactly ONE\n  never-seen field per day: grep the new group's ids against the solver's field table before anything else.\n- Time Trial ≈ 6-11 s; Mastery = `setN.` prefixed copies, 2 sets (7-12 s) through D7, 5 sets / 400-450 ids / 20-27 s on D8-D9;\n  artifacts are named `delivery N: evidence.zip`.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **HCFG location / encoding** -- same blob, different storage per container (`.hcfg` section, .NET FieldRVA, Go/Rust rodata,\n  base64 inside a Java/Kotlin constant pool, base64 Swift/Nim string literal in ELF `.rodata` on D8/D9): scan raw bytes for `HCFG`\n  and for `SENGR[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]*=*` (base64 of `HCFG`, std or urlsafe), decode, accept only if crc32(plain) matches AND the JSON\n  parses -- keep scanning past a failing hit (D9 Nim had two raw `HCFG` decoys); never a fixed offset.\n- **Location-as-answer (`hcfg_offset`, D9)** -- base-10 FILE offset of the first byte of the stored blob as it sits in the member\n  (D9: the `S` of the base64 text). 30696 in all 6 D9 variants (single, TT, Mastery x5) but compute it: record `m.start()` of\n  whichever scan hit (raw or base64) validated. For a raw-blob member it would presumably be the `HCFG` offset (unconfirmed).\n- **xor key / config values** -- re-roll per variant; derive from the header byte, never from the sample.\n- **Container per member** -- ELF vs PE32+ `.exe` re-rolls per member: Mastery set2 had d02 C++ as `.exe` (D3-D5, D7) and d04 Go as\n  `.exe` (D4, D5, D7); D6 set2 had both as ELF. Labels come from the header of THIS member (Go PE: format `pe`, architecture `x86-64` from Machine).\n- **Variant index per group** -- `dNN--variant-XXX` re-rolls per group and mode (D7 TT: d03-001, d04-006, d07-005 ...); sizes\n  and config values change, names/layout don't. TT == Mastery set1 bytes on D7; D8 TT re-used the D7 TT old-group indices\n  (d08 = variant-000); D9 TT == set1 for d02-d09 (d09 BR09-003) but d01 differed (TT variant-000 `8100`, all 5 Mastery sets one\n  other d01 with `mov eax, 8104`, symtab present). Mastery d09 = BR09-003/006/001/004/007; d08 stayed variant-000..004.\n- **Mastery packaging** -- `setN.` prefixed ids, each set its own `delivery N: evidence.zip` (same filename): pair by sha256.\n  Set count re-rolls: 2 (D2-D7), 5 on D8-D9 (set2/set4 had d02/d04 as PE both days): loop over every `setN.`. From D9 (all\n  lanes) the first question of each set ends `Evidence case: d09-m-<16hex>. [assigned-artifact-sha256:<zip sha256>]`\n  (TT: `d09-tt-...`): an explicit set -> delivery map; it is question TEXT, never part of an answer.\n- **answer_format.kind for the same field name** -- D8: `d01_analysis_elf_class`/`machine` exact_text (`ELF64`/`x86-64`) and\n  `d08_analysis_elf_class`/`machine` integer (`64`/`62`) in ONE contract; D9 `d09_analysis_machine` integer again. Rule:\n  dispatch on `kind`, not on the field name.\n- **Field set per old group** -- old groups gain ids over days (D8 padded every group to 10). Answer by field name from the\n  parsed facts dict of THAT member; an unknown field must log and fall through, never crash. Keep the older 56/112-id cases passing.\n  D9 kept D8's 10 ids for d01-d08 (d08: sha, architecture, elf_class, format, machine, 5 config) and added d09 with `hcfg_offset`.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| You see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `evidence.zip`, \"AES Encrypted\" | `7z x -pinfected -y evidence.zip` (Python: `pyzipper`); `sha256sum *` for every `artifact_sha256_K` (K = archive order within the group) |\n| `readelf -h -S` hint, ELF header questions | `readelf -hSW`, `readelf -sW`; `readelf -x .huntress` / `readelf -p .huntress` for the custom-section marker |\n| \"constant returned by recover_code\" / `disassembly` | `objdump -d -M intel --no-show-raw-insn` around `<recover_code>`; capstone on the FUNC symbol's st_value..st_size bytes |\n| Stripped C build, no `.symtab` | find the leaf `b8 imm32 c3` (`mov eax, imm; ret`) in `.text` (`lib.binary.recover_return_constants`) and check it against what `main` inlines |\n| C++ / \"mangled\" / \"vtable\" / \"RTTI\" | `nm -C` / `c++filt`; look for `_ZTV<class>` (vtable), `_ZTI`/`_ZTS` (typeinfo), `_ZNK<cls>5scoreEPKc`; the PE COFF symtab: `objdump -t file.exe` |\n| `PE32+` MinGW `.exe` | `objdump -t` (COFF symbols + string table), `pefile` (Machine, OPTIONAL_HEADER.Magic, sections incl. `.hcfg`) |\n| \"CLI metadata\", `.dll` + `runtimeconfig.json`, `BSJB` in strings | `ilspycmd file.dll` (decompile to C#; installed 2026-10-07) or Python `dnfile`; config = `<PrivateImplementationDetails>` FieldRVA static array; `strings` shows `BSJB v4.0.30319` |\n| Go (\"build info, function names, file paths\") | `go version -m file` (buildinfo: go1.x, GOARCH); `lib.binary.go_buildinfo` / `go_pclntab_funcs` (stripped names survive in `.gopclntab`) |\n| Rust (\"mangled symbols with a distinct accent\", panic strings) | `readelf -hS` + `HCFG` scan was enough on D5; demangle with `rustfilt` (installed) or `lib.binary.rustfilt` (binary) if symbols are asked |\n| `.jar` / \"constant pool\" | unzip the jar, read `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF` Main-Class, `javap -v -p Main.class`; look for `ConstantValue: String` (`static final String CONFIG`) |\n| Kotlin fat jar (`kotlin/` runtime, `META-INF/main.kotlin_module`, `X$fn$1 extends ContinuationImpl` with `int label`) | Same as `.jar`: parse the Main-Class first, skip `kotlin/`/`org/jetbrains/`. Coroutine \"state machine\" = `invokeSuspend` switching on `label`; branch logic is plain `javap -c` (`lookupswitch` on String.hashCode + `ldc` results) |\n| Swift ELF (`swift5_*` sections, `$s<len><Module>...` symbols, clang `swiftlang` in `.comment`, huge 60+ MB static stdlib) | `nm FILE \\| grep '\\$s[0-9]*<Module>'` (app symbols sit just after `frame_dummy`); `bin/swift-demangle`; config getter = `lea rax,[rip+X]; movabs rax,0xd0000000000001fc; ret` → literal chars at X+0x20, count = low 48 bits (0x1fc); or simply regex `SENGR` base64 |\n| Nim ELF (`NimMain`/`NimMainModule`/`PreMain`, `TM__<hash>_N` literals, `<proc>__<mod>_u<N>` mangling e.g. `encodedConfig__v000_u31`, `@m..@snim@slib@s...nim.c` / `fatal.nim` strings, `nimZeroMem`/`nimCopyMem`) | It is plain C-compiled ELF: header ids via readelf as usual. Config proc returns a string literal: `mov eax,0x1fc; lea rdx,[rip+X]` (len, `TM__` ptr); the literal is `u64 cap\\|0x4000000000000000` then the chars, so text = X+8 (file off = VA - segment base). Or regex `SENGR` |\n| Packed / high entropy / `UPX!` | `lib.binary.packer_info` → `upx -d` on a COPY (not seen yet) |\n| Real code-reversing question (not seen yet) | `rizin -q -c \"aaa; pdf @ sym.<fn>\"`; Ghidra headless `lib.binary.ghidra_decompile` (heavy, last resort) |\n\n## Day by day\n**D1 \"ELF Aware\"** (9 Qs; ~14 min, 4 wrong). Plain C ELF64 x86-64 EXEC: class, endianness, machine, e_type, `.huntress` marker,\nhas_symtab, designated symbol, recover_code constant, entry point. readelf -h/-S/-x, objdump; pyelftools + capstone in the solver.\n- Spellings were learned by rejection (see conventions table); the 3-wrong local cap hit on machine/endianness.\n- TT: 4-member \"slot\" bundle (`slot-0..3.elf`, ids `slotN_<qid>`); profiles stripped (has_symtab=false) and \"section-beacon\"\n  (designated fn renamed `inspect_section`; the renamed fn's constant was WRONG → `none`, unconfirmed).\n\n**D2 \"Mangled\"** (17 Qs; ~22 min, 5 wrong). + C++ build (ELF, or MinGW PE32+ `.exe` in variants). First HCFG sighting (`.hcfg`\nsection). `mangled_score_symbol` = mangled `<Class>::score`, `vtable_symbol` = `_ZTV<class owning score>` (an unused base has only\n_ZTI/_ZTS). PE sets failed for hours because a set POST is all-or-nothing: `pe` was right all along, the PE SYMBOL fields wanted the\nCOMDAT form `.text$<mangled>` / `.rdata$<mangled>`. A doubled backslash in the mutex (shell quoting) failed the first batch.\n\n**D3 \"Table Manners\"** (24 Qs; ~1 h 48 min, ~60 wrong, costliest day). + .NET 8 PE32 i386 dll. `dnfile` → BSJB `metadata_version`\n(v4.0.30319) and the FieldRVA HCFG blob; machine `i386`. New d01 JSON fields section_names / symbols / disassembly (forms in the\ntable). `d03_analysis_format` = `pe_cli` after ~50 rejected synonyms (73 turns + operator probing): compose labels from spec tokens.\n\n**D4 \"Go Fish\"** (32 Qs; ~1.5 min, 1 wrong). New d04 = static, stripped Go ELF (go1.25 buildinfo). Reused the D3 solver unchanged for d01-d03.\n- `go version -m` → GOARCH; HCFG blob sat in Go rodata (magic scan). `d04_analysis_architecture`: `amd64` (GOARCH) REJECTED → `x86-64`\n  (ELF e_machine vocabulary). Lesson: \"architecture\" uses the container machine words, not the toolchain token.\n\n**D5 \"Rust Belt\"** (40 Qs; 7 s, 0 wrong, no LLM). New d05 = static Rust ELF with debug_info. The D4 composite solver passed all 40\nunchanged: format `elf`, architecture `x86-64`, HCFG config by magic scan. The Rust-specific theme (v0/legacy mangling, panic strings,\ntrait objects) was never asked; there is still no Rust-aware path (see reference/HARDENING.md).\n\n**D6 \"Pool Party\"** (48 Qs; ~5 min, 1 wrong). New d06 = `d06--variant-000.jar` (Main-Class, class major 65).\n- `7z x` the jar → `javap -v -p <Main>.class` → `static final String CONFIG` ConstantValue = base64 → HCFG blob (decodeConfig() checks the\n  magic, key=b[5], len=b[6..7], b[12+i]^key). New field `hcfg_version` = header byte 4 (base-10 integer).\n- `d06_analysis_format`: `jar_jvm` REJECTED (so the pe_cli container_runtime compound does NOT extend to Java) → `jar`.\n- Solver bug fixed: recursive unzip dissolved the .jar into META-INF/... and lost the `d06--` prefix. Keep nested .jar/.apk/.war members whole.\n- The D6 question text was REWRITTEN for formerly ambiguous fields: d01 disassembly now says \"Intel or AT&T spelling accepted\";\n  d03 format says \"one container_runtime token; CLI, CLR or .NET accepted; separators/case accepted\". Read each question's full text and `syntax`.\n\n**D7 \"Routine Check\"** (56 Qs; 3 min start→CORRECT, 1 POST, 0 wrong). New d07 = `d07--variant-000.jar`, a 5 MB Kotlin fat jar\n(Main-Class `Day07Variant000`, class major 65, ~3300 entries incl. the kotlin/ runtime). d01-d06 bytes identical to D6 (sha256 match).\n- `javap -v -p Day07Variant000.class` → `private static final String CONFIG` ConstantValue base64 → HCFG (ver 1, key 0x31, len 368,\n  crc32 checked). Answers: format `jar` (again), config values verbatim, hcfg_version `1`. Copied the D6 solver; only change: jar\n  scan visits the Main-Class first and runtime packages last, returning at the first valid HCFG (D6 code scanned every class).\n- Unasked \"state machine\": `staged$1 extends ContinuationImpl {int label; Object L$0}`; `main` reads env `HUNTRESS_HOST_MODEL`\n  (default `workstation`) → `evaluate()` lookupswitch: workstation→`success`/13 events, analysis→`decoy`/7, sandbox→`failure`/3,\n  else `failure`/1; prints trace JSON `huntress.binary.dynamic_trace.v1` {branch, event_count, campaign, family\n  `loopback_stage_transfer`}. If branch/event questions appear: read them statically from `javap -c`, never run the jar.\n- TT 5.8 s of an 11 s window, Mastery 6.4 s of 12 s, both first-try CORRECT (set2 had d02/d04 as PE `.exe`).\n\n**D8 \"Swift Justice\"** (80 Qs). New d08 = 67 MB static Swift ELF (~290k symbols, not stripped); old groups backfilled to 10 ids.\n- **Read `answer_format.kind` first:** d08 elf_class/machine were `integer` → bit width `64` (not EI_CLASS `2`) and `62`.\n- Eager `pyelftools` `iter_symbols()` and the headerless-XOR brute force each took 60 s+ on it (worker lost 120 s + a 66 s\n  profile). Recon with `readelf`/`nm`/`strings`; config = base64 HCFG in `.rodata` (`strings -n 40 | grep -m3 '^SENGR'`).\n- Mastery: 5 deliveries x ~31 MB; download ~4.6-10 s + solve ~8 s inside a 27 s window.\n- **Mastery mutated a carry-over ELF:** a STRIPPED d01 (no .symtab). The question says \"If that symbol is absent, submit []\";\n  the old \"last `mov eax, imm32; ret` leaf\" fallback answered `[\"mov eax, 8102\",\"ret\"]` and failed all 5 sets. Absent →\n  `[]`, has_symtab `false`, symbols `[]`. Never substitute a heuristic for an absence the question defines.\n- LIB: `lib.elf.fields(data)[1]` returns `elf_class_int`/`machine_int` for `kind == \"integer\"`.\n\n**D9 \"Nimble\"** (90 Qs; 86 s start→CORRECT, 1 POST, 0 wrong). New d09 = `d09--variant-000.elf`, a 60 KB Nim-compiled ELF64\nx86-64 EXEC (dynamic glibc, `.symtab` kept, 333 symbols, no `.comment`). d01-d08 answered unchanged by the copied D8 solver (89/90).\n- Recon (worker, 6 tool calls): `grep -abo HCFG` → 2 hits (30633 = assert text ``raw[0 .. 3] == \"HCFG\"``, 30664 = the Nim literal\n  `\"HCFG\"` used by the magic check; neither decodes); `grep -ao 'SENGR[A-Za-z0-9+/=_-]*'` → 508-char base64 → HCFG ver 1,\n  key 0x51; `objdump -d -M intel --start-address=...` on `encodedConfig__v000_u31` (`mov eax,0x1fc; lea rdx,[rip+..] <TM__..._25>; ret`)\n  and `decodeConfig__v000_u33` confirmed the layout. Unasked: config keys ipv4/paths/service/host_model and Nim procs\n  `evaluate__v000_u7` / `campaign__v000_u82` (D7-style branch logic; read statically if ever asked).\n- New field `d09_analysis_hcfg_offset` (`kind: integer`) = **30696 = 0x77e8 = file offset of the base64 text's first byte**\n  (literal struct at 0x77e0 = 8-byte cap `fc01..0040`, VA 0x4077e8). Solver change: record `m.start()` of the validated\n  `SENGR` match. Answers: format `elf`, architecture `x86-64`, machine `62` (integer).\n- Lost time: one jq call selected `.answer_id` (null); contract questions use `.id`.\n- TT 10 s window: 6.95 s in-window (download 3.45, solve 2.92), CORRECT first try. Mastery 20 s window, 5 sets / 450 ids:\n  12.87 s in-window (download 6.62, solve 5.38), CORRECT first try.\n\n## GM answer conventions learned\n| Field family | Accepted form | Rejected (never resubmit) |\n|---|---|---|\n| elf_class (`answer_format.kind` exact_text) | `ELF64` / `ELF32` (readelf style) | ELFCLASS64 |\n| elf_class (`kind: integer`, D8 `d08_analysis_elf_class`) | the bit width `64` / `32` (single, TT, Mastery x5) | `2` / `1` (raw EI_CLASS byte; GM-rejected D8) |\n| machine (`kind: integer`, D8-D9 `d08`/`d09_analysis_machine`) | decimal e_machine (`62` = x86-64; read u16 at offset 18 in EI_DATA order) | — |\n| endianness | `little` / `big` | little endian, lsb, msb |\n| machine (ELF) | `x86-64`, `x86`, `AArch64`, `ARM` | EM_X86_64, x86_64, amd64 |\n| machine (PE FILE_HEADER) | 0x14c → `i386`; 0x8664 → `x86-64` | x86 (for PE i386); raw `0x8664` |\n| architecture | the same machine words (Go ELF amd64 → `x86-64`; Go PE `.exe` 0x8664 → `x86-64`) | amd64, arm64, 386, x86_64 |\n| elf_type | the constant `ET_EXEC` / `ET_DYN` | EXEC |\n| entry_point, crc32_hex, xor_key_hex | lowercase hex with `0x` (crc `0x%08x`, key `0x%02x`) | — |\n| recover_code | base-10 integer (`mov eax, 0x1fa4` → 8100); `none` if the named function is absent and the format allows it (inferred) | the renamed function's constant |\n| has_symtab / has_cpp_* | enum `true`/`false` | — |\n| marker | exact `.huntress` string without the NUL; `none` if the section is missing | — |\n| format (native) | lowercase container: `elf`, `pe` (`macho` presumed) | ELF, ELF64, elf64-x86-64, HCFG, json, exe, pe32+, coff, mz |\n| format (.NET) | `pe_cli` | ~50 synonyms (pe, pe32, dotnet, .net, cil, msil, clr, managed, assembly, ...) — lib/knowledge.py DOTNET_FORMAT_REJECTED |\n| format (Java/Kotlin jar) | `jar` (D6 Java, D7 Kotlin) | jar_jvm |\n| format / architecture (Swift D8, Nim D9 ELF) | `elf` / `x86-64`; D9 machine integer `62` (source language never changes the container label) | — |\n| C++ symbols (ELF) | bare Itanium mangled name (`_ZNK...`, `_ZTV...`) | the demangled form |\n| C++ symbols (PE/MinGW) | COMDAT section name `.text$<mangled>` (function), `.rdata$<mangled>` (vtable) | bare mangled name |\n| section_names (JSON) | pyelftools order INCLUDING the leading `\"\"` | list without \"\" |\n| symbols (JSON) | non-empty .symtab names, table order, dupes kept | list with the \"\" entries |\n| disassembly (JSON) | capstone intel `\"mov eax, 8100\"`, `\"ret\"`, decimal immediates (D6 text says AT&T is also accepted) | hex imm, `\"ret \"`, `mov eax,0x..` |\n| metadata_version | BSJB root string `v4.0.30319` | net8.0 / TFM / assembly version |\n| config_* | values verbatim from the decoded HCFG JSON (mutex keeps ONE backslash) | JSON-escaped `\\\\` |\n| hcfg_version | header byte 4 as a decimal integer | — |\n| hcfg_offset (`kind: integer`, D9) | decimal FILE offset of the stored blob's first byte (base64 text start: `30696`), single+TT+Mastery x5 | — (not tried: VA 0x4077e8, struct 0x77e0, `strings -t d` 30695) |\n| xor_key_hex | HCFG header byte 5 as `0x%02x` (D8: 0x51/0x61/0x71/0x31 for d04-d07) | — |\n| compression_methods (jar, `kind: json`) | sorted distinct ZIP `compress_type` ids, compact: `[0,8]` | — |\nNaming law (from playbook.py): native format = container only; a managed .NET binary = container_runtime from the spec tokens (pe+cli).\nJava broke the compound law (`jar`), so when a label is new, try the plain container name AND the compound, one per POST.\n\n## Pitfalls that cost time\n- **A multi-field POST is all-or-nothing.** One wrong field fails the set and hides which field was wrong. On D2 this made the correct `pe`\n  look rejected for hours (the real error was the symbol form). Submit unconfirmed/new fields ALONE; batch only the known ones.\n- **Guessing synonyms for a synthetic label** (D3 .NET format: ~50 tries, ~1.5 h). Derive from spec tokens and the playbook naming laws,\n  use `hctf labels` (it ranks candidates from the playbook), and read the question `syntax` first: the GM has started embedding hints.\n- **Shell/JSON quoting** of `Global\\Huntress...` mutexes: build the submit JSON with python/jq, not by hand in a shell string.\n- **capstone `ret` has an empty op_str**: `f\"{m} {o}\"` gives `\"ret \"`. Strip it, and convert immediates to decimal.\n- **Cumulative zip**: check whether the old groups' sha256 match a previous day's confirmed bytes. If yes, those answers carry over;\n  spend the time on the NEW group only (D4-D6 took minutes because of this).\n- **Recursive unzip** destroys nested containers (.jar/.apk) and their `dNN--` prefix; keep them whole.\n- **Same field name, different `kind`** (D8 elf_class/machine): a solver keyed only on the field name emits `ELF64` for an integer\n  question. Read `answer_format.kind` per id.\n- **Huge members** (D8: 67 MB Swift static-stdlib ELF): any per-key xor brute force or per-field re-parse blows a 10 s window.\n  Cheap encodings first (raw magic, base64 magic `SENGR`), crc-gate every hit, parse each member once.\n- **Decoy magics** (D9 Nim): the code that CHECKS the magic embeds `\"HCFG\"` (and an assert message) as literals, so the first\n  raw hit is not the blob. A decoder that returns on the first crc-\"valid\" hit (empty len 0 => crc32(b\"\")==0 passes!) stops\n  early; require non-empty JSON that parses, then keep scanning, then try the base64 form.\n- **`strings -t d` offsets on Nim/Swift literals**: the byte before the text can be printable (Nim's strlit flag 0x40 = `@`), so\n  `strings` reports offset-1 (30695). Take offsets from `grep -abo`/`re.finditer(...).start()` on the exact token.\n- **Jar members are deflated**: `grep HCFG file.jar` finds nothing. Inflate the class, read constant-pool strings, base64-decode.\n  In a fat jar (Kotlin/Scala/shaded) scan the Main-Class first; a full scan of ~3000 runtime classes eats timed windows.\n- **Stripped builds** in variants (D1 slot, D2 Mastery set2, D8 Mastery d01): no recover_code symbol. Answer what the question\n  defines for absence (`[]` for the disassembly, `none` where allowed), has_symtab=false. The old leaf `mov eax, imm; ret` guess\n  failed all 5 D8 Mastery sets.\n- **Renamed designated function** (D1 TT section-beacon): the question still says recover_code. Answer `none` (when the format allows it), not the other function's constant.\n- **Tool parity** (2026-10-07): ilspycmd, dotnet, rustfilt, GoReSym (`GoReSym -t -p file`), rabin2 are installed in\n  `bin/`; monodis/de4dot are not (dnfile covers .NET metadata).\n- **Timed windows are ~6-12 s** (TT; D9 TT 10 s, Mastery 20-27 s for 5 sets, download is ~half of it): D2 TT/Mastery attempts expired from download/orchestration latency, not solver speed (the solver ran in 0.3-0.5 s).\n  After editing hctf/*.py, restart the orchestrator. D1/D2 runs used stale modules and kept sending only the slot0 ids.\n- **PE machine map must include 0x8664 → `x86-64`**: a from-scratch replay once emitted `0x8664` and failed TT twice.\n","numLines":201,"startLine":1,"totalLines":201}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016Unt54AMTEQk7HthuVTiHK","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
